Fix DaemonThreadPoolExecutor on Python 3.14.

tools/daemon_pool.py reimplements ThreadPoolExecutor._adjust_thread_count to
get daemon workers that are not registered in
concurrent.futures.thread._threads_queues, so a wedged worker cannot hold
interpreter exit open. Its own comment says it "Mirrors CPython's
implementation (3.8-3.13)" -- and that is the problem: 3.14 replaced the
executor's _initializer/_initargs attributes with a per-worker context
factory built in prepare_context(), and reordered _worker's parameters from
(executor_reference, work_queue, initializer, initargs) to
(executor_reference, ctx, work_queue).

On 3.14 the mirrored code therefore raises

  AttributeError: 'DaemonThreadPoolExecutor' object has no attribute '_initializer'

on the first submit, which takes out every concurrent tool call (terminal,
read_file, search_files, execute_code, todo) since they all run through this
pool.

Upstream caps requires-python at <3.14 rather than supporting it, so this is
not fixed upstream. Branch on sys.version_info instead of picking one API, so
the module keeps working on the 3.12/3.13 targets in PYTHON_COMPAT.

Not upstreamable as-is while their supported range excludes 3.14.

--- a/tools/daemon_pool.py	2026-08-24 14:36:23.077200399 +0200
+++ b/tools/daemon_pool.py	2026-08-24 14:36:23.077796844 +0200
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 
 from __future__ import annotations
 
+import sys
 import threading
 import weakref
 from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor
@@ -49,15 +50,27 @@
         num_threads = len(self._threads)
         if num_threads < self._max_workers:
             thread_name = "%s_%d" % (self._thread_name_prefix or self, num_threads)
-            t = threading.Thread(
-                name=thread_name,
-                target=_worker,
-                args=(
+            if sys.version_info >= (3, 14):
+                # 3.14 moved initializer/initargs behind a per-worker context
+                # factory (prepare_context) and reordered _worker's params to
+                # (executor_reference, ctx, work_queue). Reading
+                # self._initializer here raises AttributeError on 3.14.
+                args = (
+                    weakref.ref(self, weakref_cb),
+                    self._create_worker_context(),
+                    self._work_queue,
+                )
+            else:
+                args = (
                     weakref.ref(self, weakref_cb),
                     self._work_queue,
                     self._initializer,
                     self._initargs,
-                ),
+                )
+            t = threading.Thread(
+                name=thread_name,
+                target=_worker,
+                args=args,
                 daemon=True,
             )
             t.start()
